AI Prototype Summary
Model used: Gemini 3.6 Thinking

AI Chat Transcript


User Prompt

Write SystemVerilog HDL to leverage the internal high speed oscillator in the Lattice UP5K FPGA and blink an LED at 2 Hz. Take full advantage of SystemVerilog syntax, for example, using logic instead of wire and reg.



AI Response

// Top-level module to blink an LED at 2 Hz using the iCE40 UltraPlus internal oscillator
module led_blinker_2hz (
    output logic led = 1'b0
);

    // Internal clock signal generated by the high-speed oscillator primitive
    logic clk_48m;

    // Instantiate Lattice iCE40 UP5K High-Speed Oscillator primitive (SB_HFOSC)
    // CLKHF_DIV configuration:
    //   "0b00" -&gt; 48 MHz
    //   "0b01" -&gt; 24 MHz
    //   "0b10" -&gt; 12 MHz
    //   "0b11" -&gt;  6 MHz
    SB_HFOSC #(
        .CLKHF_DIV("0b00")  // Configure oscillator output to 48 MHz
    ) hfosc_inst (
        .CLKHFPU(1'b1),     // Power up the oscillator (1 = active)
        .CLKHFEN(1'b1),     // Enable output clock signal (1 = active)
        .CLKHF(clk_48m)     // Clock output port
    );

    // Counter frequency math:
    // To achieve a 2 Hz blink rate (period = 0.5 s), the LED toggles twice per second (every 0.25 s).
    // Toggles every N cycles: N = f_clk / (2 * f_target) = 48,000,000 / (2 * 2) = 12,000,000
    localparam int CLK_FREQ_HZ    = 48_000_000;
    localparam int TARGET_FREQ_HZ = 2;
    localparam int TOGGLE_COUNT   = (CLK_FREQ_HZ / (2 * TARGET_FREQ_HZ)) - 1;

    // 24-bit counter required to reach 11,999,999 (2^24 = 16,777,216)
    logic [23:0] counter = '0;

    // Sequential counter and LED state toggle block
    always_ff @(posedge clk_48m) begin
        if (counter == TOGGLE_COUNT) begin
            counter &lt;= '0;
            led     &lt;= ~led;
        end else begin
            counter &lt;= counter + 1'b1;
        end
    end

endmodule
Design Details & SystemVerilog Syntax Lattice Primitive (SB_HFOSC): The SB_HFOSC primitive is the hard macro for the high-speed oscillator on iCE40 UltraPlus (UP5K) FPGAs. It outputs a nominal 48 MHz clock signal when .CLKHF_DIV(“0b00”) is selected. If using Lattice Radiant IP modules, this primitive may also be wrapped under the HSOSC component name.
logic Data Type: Replaces legacy wire and reg declarations, eliminating ambiguity between net and register types across internal signals and port declarations.
always_ff procedural block: Enforces register synthesis intent for sequential clock-driven logic.
localparam int and Literal formatting: Uses integer-typed constants with literal underscores (48_000_000) for clear clock arithmetic readability.
Fill literal (’0): SystemVerilog syntax to reset all bits of a vector without explicitly sizing 24’d0.



User Prompt

Your code failed to synthesize.



AI Response

The synthesis failure on the Lattice iCE40 UltraPlus (UP5K) is almost certainly caused by one of three issues specific to Lattice synthesis engines (LSE/Synplify Pro) or your chosen toolchain flow:

Toolchain Primitive Name Mismatch (SB_HFOSC vs. HSOSC) Lattice Radiant: Uses the primitive name HSOSC. If you instantiate SB_HFOSC in Radiant, synthesis throws an unknown module error.

iCEcube2 / Yosys / Open-Source (nextpnr): Uses the primitive name SB_HFOSC.

Inline Port & Variable Initialization Declarations like output logic led = 1’b0; or logic [23:0] counter = ’0; are valid in SystemVerilog simulation, but many FPGA synthesizers (especially Lattice LSE) reject inline initializers on port declarations or variables. Initialization should be handled via an initial block (for power-up defaults) or an explicit reset signal.
Strict SystemVerilog Synthesizer Frontends Certain LSE or older Synplify versions fail on unsized literals (’0) or typed local parameters (localparam int).

Universal, Synthesis-Safe SystemVerilog Code For Open-Source (Yosys/nextpnr) or Lattice iCEcube2:
module led_blinker_2hz (
    output logic led
);

    logic clk_48m;

    // Hard primitive for iCEcube2 / Yosys open-source flow
    SB_HFOSC #(
        .CLKHF_DIV("0b00")  // 48 MHz output
    ) hfosc_inst (
        .CLKHFPU(1'b1),
        .CLKHFEN(1'b1),
        .CLKHF(clk_48m)
    );

    // 48 MHz / (2 * 2 Hz) = 12,000,000 cycles per toggle
    localparam [23:0] TOGGLE_COUNT = 24'd11_999_999;

    logic [23:0] counter;

    // Power-on state initialization for FPGA synthesis
    initial begin
        counter = 24'd0;
        led     = 1'b0;
    end

    always_ff @(posedge clk_48m) begin
        if (counter == TOGGLE_COUNT) begin
            counter &lt;= 24'd0;
            led     &lt;= ~led;
        end else begin
            counter &lt;= counter + 1'b1;
        end
    end

endmodule
For Lattice Radiant: If using Lattice Radiant, replace the primitive instantiation block above with HSOSC:
   // Hard primitive for Lattice Radiant software
    HSOSC #(
        .CLKHF_DIV("0b00")  // 48 MHz output
    ) hfosc_inst (
        .CLKHFPU(1'b1),
        .CLKHFEN(1'b1),
        .CLKHF(clk_48m)
    );
Synthesis Checklist File Extension & Tool Settings: Ensure your file is saved as .sv (not .v). If using iCEcube2 or Radiant, ensure the tool project options are set to SystemVerilog mode rather than Verilog-2001.
Library Inclusions: If using Radiant, ensure the target family is set to iCE40UP so the HSOSC macro is visible to the synthesizer.



Summary
When I asked Gemini to implement the LED blinker at first, there is one obvious mistake in its code:
module led_blinker_2hz (
    output logic led = 1'b0
);
Gemini declared an initialization value directly inside the blinker module’s output port list, which is illegal. Thus, the code quality of Gemini remains questionable, even though the rest of the code looks clean and well-documented. However, when I told Gemini that its code did not synthesize, even given this minimal information, Gemini was able to pinpoint the issue and fix its own code in one go. This proves that the model has a strong debugging ability. Therefore, in the future, I’d be curious to see what AI can do as a debugging buddy, as it seems to have quite some potential.

AI Prototype Summary
Model used: Claude Fable 5.1

AI Chat Transcript


User Prompt

Write SystemVerilog HDL to time multiplex a single seven segment decoder (that decodes from four bits to a common anode seven segment display) to decode two sets of input bits and drive two sets of seven output bits.



AI Response

The file contains two modules: hex_to_7seg (the single combinational decoder, active-low for common anode) and tdm_seven_seg, which toggles a sel bit each clock, muxes din0/din1 into the one decoder, and latches the result into seg0 or seg1. Each output holds its value and refreshes every other cycle, so the displays never flicker even though only one decoder exists.
If you want a real chip-level multiplexed display (one shared segment bus plus per-digit anode enables) instead of two independent 7-bit outputs, that’s a small variation — let me know.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// hex_to_7seg
//   Combinational 4-bit -&gt; 7-segment decoder for a COMMON-ANODE display.
//   Segments are active-LOW (0 = lit).  Bit order: seg[6:0] = {g,f,e,d,c,b,a}
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
module hex_to_7seg (
    input  logic [3:0] bin,
    output logic [6:0] seg      // {g,f,e,d,c,b,a}, active low
);
    always_comb begin
        unique case (bin)      //        gfedcba
            4'h0: seg = 7'b1000000;
            4'h1: seg = 7'b1111001;
            4'h2: seg = 7'b0100100;
            4'h3: seg = 7'b0110000;
            4'h4: seg = 7'b0011001;
            4'h5: seg = 7'b0010010;
            4'h6: seg = 7'b0000010;
            4'h7: seg = 7'b1111000;
            4'h8: seg = 7'b0000000;
            4'h9: seg = 7'b0010000;
            4'hA: seg = 7'b0001000;
            4'hB: seg = 7'b0000011;
            4'hC: seg = 7'b1000110;
            4'hD: seg = 7'b0100001;
            4'hE: seg = 7'b0000110;
            4'hF: seg = 7'b0001110;
            default: seg = 7'b1111111;   // all off
        endcase
    end
endmodule


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// tdm_seven_seg
//   Time-multiplexes ONE hex_to_7seg instance between two 4-bit inputs.
//   Each clock the mux alternates inputs; the decoder result is latched
//   into the matching output register, so both 7-bit outputs are held
//   continuously and each refreshes every other clock.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
module tdm_seven_seg (
    input  logic       clk,
    input  logic       rst_n,      // async active-low reset
    input  logic [3:0] din0,
    input  logic [3:0] din1,
    output logic [6:0] seg0,       // decoded din0, active low
    output logic [6:0] seg1        // decoded din1, active low
);
    logic       sel;               // 0 -&gt; din0 this cycle, 1 -&gt; din1
    logic [3:0] bin_mux;
    logic [6:0] seg_dec;

    // Input mux feeding the single decoder
    assign bin_mux = sel ? din1 : din0;

    hex_to_7seg u_dec (
        .bin (bin_mux),
        .seg (seg_dec)
    );

    // Output demux: capture decoder result into the selected output register
    always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n) begin
        if (!rst_n) begin
            sel  &lt;= 1'b0;
            seg0 &lt;= 7'b1111111;    // blank on reset
            seg1 &lt;= 7'b1111111;
        end else begin
            if (sel) seg1 &lt;= seg_dec;
            else     seg0 &lt;= seg_dec;
            sel &lt;= ~sel;
        end
    end
endmodule

